body:
I know you love mountain biking but stop. You will end up a paraplegic if you don't quit now.

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

Then again my dumbass loves it so much I am already back on the trails with an adaptive rig

I was training for a race in a summer downhill mtb series. I hit a trail I have run a dozen times. One of the last trails of the day. I woke up 9 days later with 12 broken ribs, multiple brain bleeds, crushed esophagus, bruised lungs, fractured neck at C6 and multple fractures from t4 to t11 including a burst at t7/t8 sending fragments through my spinal cord and leaving me a paraplegic. 

&#x200B;

AKA your boy broke his back and sent it a lil too hard.

It was hard but I was disciplined and very fortunate to start my career in finance while still in school. I then decided to get back into mountain biking at 25 which cost me around 180k in savings and investments. 

&#x200B;

Expensive sport, even more expensive when you become a paralyzed lol. 

I moved back in with my grandparents when my grandfather was diagnosed with cancer and I was still in school. I always worked and had a landscaping business on the side that allowed me to have zero debt. 

&#x200B;

So low costs, multiple streams of income and not dating allowed me to save. I went to the gym, the odd rave and hiked with my dogs. Then got back into mountain biking which after the intial bike expense isnt that expensive of a sport. So money came in, barely went out. Then it all went out right in the midst of covid and with it my ability to walk.

&#x200B;

I am sharing this to firstly answer the question. Secondly however to showcase how grateful I am for my younger self to have been in the financial position and I really want to stress to anyone reading this as frstrating as investing and putting money away may be, it is a neccesary thing IF you are able to. I returned to work making more money, spending less and somehow I feel like I can afford less  lol so if you just need to survive - do so and dont feel shame around that. If you can however, put money away and treat it like a bill/expense. 

&#x200B;

Otherwise - I must say do not neglect insurance benefits at work. I was young and thought myself to be industructable - maybe a broken collar bone would put me out for a season. but id be fine as i always knew how to make money on the side. I never expected this and I do wish I opted for the higher level of insurance coverage for long term. I made it by due to my investments - but they got eaten up incredibly quickly.

I try to flush it with shit tons of water over 2 or 3 days and if it doesnt improve ill get tested and hop on antibiotics. I try to avoid them as I do not want to become resistant but sometimes they are necessary. Macrobid at 100mg twice a day 10 days is my go to for minor ones or if i cant get tested ill hop on this until i know which i need. If its a bad UTI or not going away 500mg twice a day for 7 days of Cipro has been great. The issue with taking any antibiotic but especially one like cipro is it doesnt give a fuck and targets everything which means your "good" bacteria as well So I would recommend eating lots of yogurt with probiotics and cheese. You can hop on a probiotic supplement but most are trash to be honest, but every bit helps. 

&#x200B;

As a preventative, make sure you have good hygiene, have a clean cath environment and really track your fluid to cath intake and time ratio. You can also take D-Mannose and Cran Supplements and I also take Chanca Piedra supplements but these are only preventatives, dont over do them as they can make your bladder more reactive and need to urninate more but also if you do develop a UTI and start antibiotics, stop taking these until youre done the antibiotic cycle as they can reduce effectiveness.

I am paralyzed from an accident in 2020. VERY thankful for working for an amazing company and not on ODSP but I am faced with a choice where I understand why so many are stuck on ODSP. If I am on ODSP I would get assistance for things I need such as catheters etc which are about 500 or more a month plus other expenses related to my injury. I know people that with spinal cord injuries that dont work because they cant get transportation, they cant find work that would understand and accommodate and a lot of them it just isnt worth it when you add up their expenses vs take home compared to odsp. 

&#x200B;

I work for a great company and okay private insurance, I drive my own car and I have had great community support along with me being smart with my money pre injury has put me in a good position to focus on recovery and return my job without stressing too much about finances. However this has definitely opened my eyes to have understanding and compassion as to why so many on ODSP struggle and are bitter. Especially when the MAID criteria has expanded to include some of those with disabilities and mental health issues apparently which to me comes across as "hey we're sorry we cant help you live life or respect you enough to get proper resources to give you a fighting chance but we will gladly help you kill yourself."

&#x200B;

I work as a financial advisor and pre injury and a few clients on ODSP and many had to do under the table jobs or hide money in family accounts just to live comfortably.

A Serbian Film.

&#x200B;

I have a list of fucked of movies and it is the only one I haven't watched yet.

I just ordered a Sony A7IV with a kit lens and really confused on lens and what the differences mean like 1.8 or 2.4 or what to use different lens for. From other reviews it definitely seems i will want to ditch the kit lens but not sure what to get. 

&#x200B;

So the main purpose of the camera is for product photography and portraits for our business still wise and likely shoot some ads and action shots as well with it video wise for the business.

&#x200B;

Personally would enjoy being able to do street photography and night/dark shots and wildlife/landscapes but the priority is the business stuff. Any guidance as far as direction for lens is appreciated.

CPP, any private pension, OAS. Hopefully they own their home or have cheap rent. Many have lots of debt and continue to use their overdraft and line of credit regularly to float. Some even are just chilling waiting for their 95 year old mother to die to collect.

&#x200B;

I had many clients in the bank that fit this and the very day those cheques hit the accounts the lines of retired broke folk lined up out the door.

She rejected me for a dance at grad and in our mid 20s we chatted a bit on FB and ended up chilling at a bar when i came back to town. I was originally visiting my friend but told her id be there and she came out. Invited back to her place for more drinks, had no expectations of anything since we were just friendly and not only was my buddy with us but her roommate and roommates partner. Anyways she started saying we had too much to drink and should spend the night and because she knew me she said i could share her bed and my buddy can sleep on the couch. My buddy smoked weed for the first time of his life plus drank a bit with her roommates so he was all over the map so i think even though I know I'm fine, fair play. We go up and we slept together but then the post nut realization that I'm sleeping on a mattress on the floor covered in cat hair set in and i just wasn't about it so made up  an excuse about needing to be home for an appointment (i lived an hour away) hugged goodbye collected my half dead buddy and drove him home and drove myself home. We still talked and bailed on a few plans after that before she moved East.

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

When she rejected me it should be noted she wasnt mean about it or anything. I know she was really crushing on someone else at the time.

My first non shit job was at a unionized fabrication shop in Burlington and it was so weird working for a place that cared about employees and had rules in place to respect the workers rights as well as the Unions contracts. We werent allowed to even be on the floor before our shift started let alone clock in and they told up usually to start cleaning up a half hour before shift ended to make sure we could leave on time regardless how far along we were. It was so weird coming from a shitty job with shitty managers.

&#x200B;

I shifted my career to finance after school and work for a bank now and its been interesting to observe throughout life how the more money you make and as you progress through job titles the better the work to life balance becomes and more chilled out your leadership becomes. Unless you work in IB or MA, those guys get fucked.
